  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1125 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nLate evening infrared satellite imagery depicted an impressive\nmesoscale convective system (MCS) entering NW KS. This MCS has\nshown no signs of weakening, probably a function of excellent\noutflow aloft per cirrus on satellite, but is just now showing\nsome of the initial signs of weakening. The apex of the bowing \nsegment is moving due south as of 11 pm, and will enter the \nnorthern zones through the early morning hours while gradually \nweakening. For short term pops, followed the general track of \nthe latest 00z ARW solution, which has been one of the favored \nCAM solutions the past couple days. Increased pops greatly over \nthe current NBM dry solution. Primary risk from storms through \nearly morning will be outflow wind gusts of 50-60 mph north of \nK-96, with subsequent weakening after that and with southern \nextent. Current thinking is MCS will dissipate roughly over the \ncentral zones, around DDC, during the predawn hours. \n\nAll models show significant warming Friday, with a net warming\nof +3 to +5C at 850 mb, along with modest height rises, and much\nimproved boundary layer mixing and stronger south winds. Lee\ntroughing over eastern Colorado will support south winds of\n20-30 mph Friday afternoon with temperatures well into the 90s.\n\nA dry forecast is evident Friday and Saturday for all zones, as\nsubsidence prevails. The heat will continue to intensify\nSaturday, with another net warming of +3C at 850 mb, and more of\na SWly downslope contribution. As we have seen much of this\nsummer, NBM guidance continues to be a few degrees too cool with\nthese excessively hot days. Added 1-2 degrees across the board\nfor Saturday and Sunday afternoons, with afternoon temperatures\nof 100-105 common both days. Heat indices will remain less than\n105 Saturday, with no heat advisory planned. Apparent\ntemperatures will be near 105 across southeast zones for a few\nhours Sunday afternoon, where a heat advisory will likely be\nrequired. The hottest guidance is preferred, with 00z MOS\nguidance in the 103-105 range for DDC, and this would threaten\nrecord highs this weekend. Strong south to southwest winds are\nexpected each afternoon, 3-5 mph stronger than NBM guidance, and\nthis will amplify the blow dryer, dehydration effect for\npeople, pets and livestock outside this weekend. \n\nMidlevel high pressure over Texas Sunday will elongate into a\nlonger ridge axis early next week, but remained centered over \nthe central and southern plains. As such, afternoon temperatures\nwill remain well above normal, within a few degrees of 100 each\nday. Again, NBM is probably a few degrees too cool in this \nanomalously hot pattern for early September. Rain chances will \nbe minimal to absent through early next week, but some limited \nthunderstorm activity may clip the northwest zones at peak \nheating each day, furthest from the subsidence of the ridge \naxis, and nearer enhanced SWly flow aloft.",
